{"qid":"Q29654286","names":["Kingdom of Cappadocia"],"title":"Kingdom of Cappadocia","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Cappadocia developed from an Achaemenid satrapy in central Anatolia, with the local dynast Ariarathes I (d. 322 BCE) the first to assert independence from the Persians and then resist the Macedonians. Three ruling houses governed in succession—the Ariarathids to 96 BCE, the Ariobarzanids to 36 BCE, and finally Archelaus (r. 36 BCE–17 CE), who ruled under Roman protection. The kingdom maintained a strong Iranian cultural identity, its aristocracy honoring Persian fire-temples and titles, centuries after Achaemenid rule ended. When Archelaus died in Rome in 17 CE, Emperor Tiberius converted Cappadocia directly into a Roman province, citing the king's inability to govern.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The general extent of the Kingdom of Cappadocia in central Anatolia is well established through ancient geographers and numismatic evidence, though its precise borders fluctuated with the competing claims of Pontus and Rome.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Cappadocia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Cappadocia"},{"label":"Britannica: Cappadocia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Cappadocia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ariarathes I","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ariarathes_I"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Cappadocia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ariarathes III","nativeName":"Ἀριαράθης Γ΄","roman":"Ariarathes III","start":-255,"end":-220,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"